> It doesn't fit logically.

There's no logical reason for the pc to have forgotten his stuff, but
it nonetheless makes for an entertaining idea.

after all, the player will most likely have forgotten some stuff about
the pc, so its only natural that the pc will have hibernated for
awhile.

In game logic, the pc might suddenly realise that his potent memories
pertinent to the quest he is on have become vague and uncertain - maybe
he has an out of body experience and forgets who he is.

Here's some further thoughts:

The pc should have his hp and power taken up to maximum as the effects
of amnesia occur.
Perhaps he should have the effects of hunger removed too.  In these
ideas I assume that the pc was sleeping in a sheltered spot, thus being
in a good regenerative state.

Corpses recently killed at the time of saving should go rotten and be
unsuitable for eating or sacrificing.

Possibly peaceful monsters on the level should 'wander off'?  Tame pets
could slowly lose their tameness.  Losing a treasured pet would be even
nastier than a level.

Shopkeepers should have restocked their shelves with new merchandise
slowly, too.

All bad effects of this gradual amnesia should be curable - potions of
restore abilities sound like a good candidate.

How quickly should this happen?  I think that maybe it should be 1
month idle = 1 spell lost + 1 level lost, with the effects continuing
per month in linear function.  But it largely depends upon the player's
style.  Maybe it should be adjustable?

Maybe there should be some good effects from this hibernation.  For
example, a mage might spend the idle time pondering the nature of
magic, getting a free max power point per month.  A priest would spend
the time in prayer, lowering his prayer timeout.  A rogue would engage
in forgery or pickpocketing, getting a few zorkmids.  A tourist might
take the time to develop the many photos he's taken.

Of course if these effects were too sweet, people might scum them by
setting their computer timers forward by a few years.

Still, these effects may have a minimal yet entertaining result upon a
long game and remind players that their pcs need constant attention.  :)



